The relevant code is at `server.js:35-38`:

```js
const port = process.env.APP_PORT;

app.listen(port, () => {
  logger.info(`Example app listening on port ${port}`);
});
```

**The bug:** `process.env.APP_PORT` is read directly with no fallback/default value. If the `APP_PORT` environment variable isn't set (e.g., missing from `.env`), `port` will be `undefined`.

In Node.js, calling `app.listen(undefined)` doesn't crash — it still starts the server, but on a **random OS-assigned port** instead of the expected port 9000. The log message will say `listening on port undefined`, and nothing will be reachable at port 9000.

The fix (when you're ready) is simply adding a default: `process.env.APP_PORT || 9000`.

